WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.970
- People may look down on these things

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.340
like a dandelion flower or cattails.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.181
They're like, "This is
just some common weed.

00:00:09.181 --> 00:00:11.487
"How can this be used by
Michelin star restaurants?"

00:00:11.487 --> 00:00:13.470
And I think that's what's changing.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:14.870
People wanna go back to things

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.330
that are sort of raw.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.913
(upbeat music)

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.083
My name is Tama Matsuoka
Wong, and I'm a forager.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.630
When I first hear the word forager,

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:40.020
I didn't really know what it meant.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.470
But then I found out
that it's sort of like

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.100
doing a dance with nature and finding

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.933
the secret foods in the wild.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.440
Most of the restaurants I work with

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.880
are Michelin star restaurants,

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.543
such as Gramercy Tavern,
restaurant Daniel,

00:00:52.921 --> 00:00:55.360
Cosme, and a number of others.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.980
I came from a different background.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.630
I didn't study horticulture.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.200
I didn't study botany.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.190
I went to Harvard Law School,
and I practiced a career

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.693
for decades in investment banking.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.010
I always liked nature;
I always liked food.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.720
I was a failed gardener so I
was trying to find good recipes

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.200
for my weeds that I found
out were good to eat.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.280
So, people just started
asking me for this.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.900
I started getting more and more involved.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.630
It was super creative, impactful,

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:23.630
and it just took me.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.720
Most of the stuff that I
get is from around my house

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.453
but you, you could find
common weeds anywhere.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.070
Probably I deal with between four

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.150
and 500 different plants a year.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.220
Some of my favorite ingredients are sumac,

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.723
wild brassica, I like wild
shiso, and cattail shoots.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.930
People don't realize that all the stuff

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:55.540
around them is good food.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.940
In two meters of ground, you
could have 50 different plants.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:00.610
Fifty, and that's nature.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.610
It can be something
amazing that you can enjoy,

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.953
and you don't even have
to go too far to get it.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.965
When I first started bringing my weeds

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:15.893
to very elevated restaurants...

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.010
- Oh, wow, that's beautiful.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:19.710
- [Tama] I didn't know you're
supposed to call chefs, Chefs

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.960
because I'm just a forager.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.410
- Well, the thing I love is that the chefs

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.360
just see the huge beauty
in every of these plants.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.060
They love them like they're little jewels.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.450
And I don't feel like it's my product;

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:36.820
I feel like it's nature's product

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.490
and that they respect the plant.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:39.550
So, it's a huge bond.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.330
It's not about the chef or me.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:42.380
It's about the plant.

